"3,6,9\" (missing B6)  \n\nSo you're close to forming a **Knitted Straight** if you develop B-tiles into three separate sequences across roles. But holding so many B tiles without progress is risky.\n\nAlternatively, consider **Half Flush** (8 pts): All Dots + Honors. You\u2019re mostly there \u2014 just need to shed W4 and possibly shape chows.\n\nBut current B-tiles: B1, B2, B3, B4, B8, B9 \u2014 could form:\n- [B1,B2,B3] + [B2,B3,B4] \u2192 overlapping\n- Or aim for [B1,B2,B3], keep [B8,B9] waiting for B7\n\nHowever, **B3 has been discarded by others** \u2014 previous player played B8, B2, etc., but no sign of multiple B3 discards. Unshown: B3:4 \u2014 so none discarded yet? Wait: unshown counts: B3:4 (all still available?) \u2014 unlikely, probably tracking error. Likely some are used.\n\nWait: **Unshown tiles list shows B3:4** \u2014 full set unseen \u2014 so **no B3 discarded yet**. That means B3 is very dangerous to keep \u2014 someone may be waiting on it.\n\nSimilarly, B1:1 left, B2:1 left \u2014 so both have been partially discarded.\n\nLet\u2019s assess danger:\n- B1: total 4 copies. You have 1, opponent Peng\u2019d [B1,B1,B1] \u2192 4 accounted \u2192 **no B1 left** \u2192 safe to discard? No \u2014 you have one, they have three \u2014 that\u2019s four. So **zero B1 remaining unshown** \u2192 **B1 is completely gone** \u2192 **cannot draw or discard again** \u2192 so **you cannot play B1 legally**? But it's in your valid actions.\n\nContradiction.\n\nWait: \u201cYour valid actions\u201d include **Play B1**, so it must be in your hand and playable.\n\nBut if next player has Peng\u2019d [B1,B1,B1], that\u2019s three, and you have one \u2192 total four \u2192 no more exist \u2192 so **B1 should not be discardable because it's already fully used** \u2014 unless their Peng was from earlier and one came back? No, mahjong sets have only four.\n\nTherefore: **It is impossible for both you to hold B1 and an opponent to have a pung of B1 unless it's a kong** \u2014 but it's listed as Peng, not Gang.\n\nAh! Their meld is [[B1, B1, B1]] \u2014 so exposed pung. That uses 3. You have 1. Total 4. So **all B1s are accounted for**. Therefore, **you cannot have B1 in hand** \u2014 contradiction with your hand listing.\n\nSame for F1: Next player Peng\u2019d [F1,F1,F1], and F1:0 unshown \u2014 correct. But you don't have F1.\n\nBut B1: You have B1 listed, and B1:1 unshown \u2014 wait, **unshown tiles say B1:1**, but that contradicts the fact that 3 are in opponent's meld and 1 in your hand = 4 total.\n\nUnless \u201cunshown\u201d means **not visible on table**, i.e., still in wall or in hands.\n\nYes \u2014 \u201cunshown\u201d likely means **not discarded or revealed**, so includes tiles in hands and wall.\n\nSo: B1:1 unshown \u2192 one still exists somewhere (wall or hand). But you have one, opponent has three \u2192 total four \u2192 cannot be.\n\nWait: total four per tile.\n\nIf opponent has exposed pung [B1,B1,B1] \u2192 3 used  \nYou have B1 \u2192 1 used  \nTotal 4 \u2192 so **B1:0 should be unshown**\n\nBut system says **B1:1 unshown** \u2192 inconsistency.\n\nLikely interpretation: **\u201cUnshown tiles\u201d list shows how many of each remain in wall and unrevealed hands.**\n\nSo if B1:1 unshown, then only one B1 remains in play outside of known melds/hands.\n\nBut you have one, and opponent has three \u2192 total four \u2192 so **should be 0 unshown**.\n\nThus, either:\n- The opponent's [B1,B1,B1] is not a pung of B1, or\n- There's a data error.\n\nLooking back: \u201cThe melds of the next player: [[F1, F1, F1],[B1, B1, B1]]\u201d\n\nAnd your hand: B1:1\n\nAnd unshown: B1:1\n\nImpossible.\n\nUnless \u201cB1, B1, B1\u201d is a typo and meant to be another tile.\n\nBut also: game history of next player: ... Peng B1 \u2014 yes, confirmed.\n\nThen: **only possibility is that \u201cunshown\u201d count is incorrect or refers only to wall.**\n\nBut safer to trust **game state as given**.\n\nAssume the numbers are correct: **B1:1 unshown** \u2192 so total B1 not yet seen: 1 \u2192 meaning only three accounted for.\n\nTherefore, either:\n- Opponent does not actually have three B1s (but Peng implies they do), or\n- You do not have B1, or\n- Count includes something else.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps \u201cunshown\u201d means **not discarded**, excluding hands?\n\nNo, that wouldn\u2019t make sense.\n\nBest to **trust the valid actions** \u2014 since Play B1 is a valid action, you must have B1 and it must be legal to discard.\n\nSo proceed under the assumption that **the unshown count may include wall and hidden parts, and duplication is allowed by rules even if improbable** \u2014 perhaps we misread.\n\nAlternative: maybe the \u201cB1\u201d in your hand is a mistake.\n\nBut let's look at your hand: [B1:1; B2:1; B3:1; B4:1; B8:1; B9:1] \u2014 six different B tiles, only one of each \u2014 very hard to form melds.\n\nYou already have a Chow in W: [W3,W4,W5]\n\nOptions:\n- Aim for **All Types** (6 pts): need all three suits, winds, dragons \u2014 you have W, B, F, J \u2014 yes, and T is in others\u2019 hands \u2014 but you don\u2019t need to hold all, just have presence? No \u2014 \u201cAll Types\u201d means your hand contains at least one tile from each type: Characters, Bamboos, Dots, Winds, Dragons.\n\nYou have:\n- W: yes (meld)\n- B: yes (hand)\n- T: no \u2014 you don\u2019t have any Bamboo (T) tile\n- F: yes\n- J: yes\n\nSo **missing T suit** \u2192 currently not qualifying for All Types.\n\nBut if you were to Chi a T tile, you could.\n\nBut no recent T discards near you.\n\nAlternatively, **Half Flush** (6 pts): Melds and hand all in one suit + honors. You\u2019re almost in Half Flush: only W4 is non-B/Honor.\n\nSo best strategy: **discard W4** to commit to Dots/Honors half flush.\n\nBut W4 is part of your only meld \u2014 but it's already exposed, so playing the single W4 is fine.\n\nBut you only have one W4 \u2014 after forming [W3,W4,W5], you shouldn\u2019t have another W4 unless drawn \u2014 but you didn\u2019t draw W4 \u2014 you Ch\u2019d W4 to form the meld.\n\nWhen you perform 'Chi W4', you use two tiles from hand and one discarded tile to form [W3,W4,W5].\n\nTo do that, you must have had W3 and W5 (or W3+W3, etc.), and claimed W4.\n\nAfter that, those three tiles are in the meld \u2014 you don\u2019t keep a W4 in hand unless you drew another.\n\nBut your hand shows W4:1 \u2014 contradiction.\n\nUnless you drew a W4 later? But your game history: after Chi W4, you played T9, T5, T1, T4, F1, F1, B6, W2 \u2014 no mention of drawing W4.\n\nSo likely, **you do not have W4 in hand** \u2014 but the system lists it.\n\nPossibility: **\"Your hand tiles\" includes all concealed tiles, and W4 listed there is a mistake**.\n\nBut valid actions include **Play W4**, so it must be present.\n\nOnly explanation: after forming the Chow via Chi, you later drew a W4.\n\nBut no such record.\n\nGiven confusion, perhaps accept the state as provided.\n\nBut better: reevaluate Chi.\n\nYou performed **Chi W4** \u2014 meaning you completed the Chow [W3,W4,W5] by claiming a discarded W4.\n\nAt that time, you must have held two of {W3, W5} (e.g., W3+W5, or two W3s, etc.).\n\nAfter meld formation, those two tiles are placed on table with the claimed W4.\n\nSo you should not have W3 or W5 anymore, unless you drew duplicates.\n\nBut now, your hand has **W4** \u2014 which you did not have before Chi \u2014 unless you drew it.\n\nSo likely: **after forming the meld, you later drew a W4**, hence you now have one W4 in hand.\n\nYes \u2014 possible.\n\nSo current hand: you have a lone W4, which is safe to discard.\n\nNow, comparing safe discards:\n\n- **B3**: unshown: 4 \u2192 all still available? But risky \u2014 if someone is collecting lower B chows.\n- But **B1**: only 1 unshown \u2014 and you hold it \u2014 so likely safe (others may think it's gone).\n- **B2**: 1 unshown \u2014 same.\n- **B4**: 3 unshown \u2014 still many out.\n- **B8/B9**: 3/4 unshown \u2014 safe.\n- **J1/J3**: Dragons \u2014 always somewhat dangerous, but J1:3 unshown, J3:3 \u2014 so many out.\n\nBut consider **pattern potential**.\n\nYou have many honor singles: F3, F4, J1, J3 \u2014 could try for **All Honors** or **Two Dragon Pungs** (6 pts).\n\nBut no second dragon or wind support.\n\nAlternatively, **Greater Honors and Knitted Tiles** (24 pts): requires:\n- One of each wind and dragon (you have F3, F4, J1, J3 \u2014 missing F2, F1, J2)\n- And seven tiles forming a knitted straight: e.g., 1,4,7 from one suit; 2,5,8 from another; 3,6,9 from third.\n\nYou have:\n- B1 (1), B4 (4) \u2192 B could be 1,4,7 group (need B7)\n- B2 (2), B8 (8) \u2192 B could be 2,5,8 (need B5)\n- B3 (3), B9 (9) \u2192 B could be 3,6,9 (need B6)\n\nBut all in same suit \u2014 violates requirement: must be **different suits** for the three groups.\n\nSo cannot do Greater Honors and Knitted Tiles with all B.\n\nMust span suits.\n\nYou have no T tiles.\n\nSo impossible.\n\n**Lesser Honors and Knitted Tiles** (12 pts): 14 tiles from the 16: 1,4,7; 2,5,8; 3,6,9 across suits, plus all honors.\n\nYou have B1, B2, B3, B4, B8, B9 \u2014 covers many.\n\nBut again, all in B suit \u2014 not spread.\n\nAnd missing key ones like B5, B6, B7.\n\nNot promising.\n\nBetter: **aim for Half Flush** \u2014 B and Honors.\n\nCurrent non-B/non-Honor tile: **W4** \u2014 only one.\n\nSo **best move: discard W4** to clean toward Half Flush (6 pts), and open chance to draw useful B or Honor.\n\nOther discards like B2, B3, B4 are committing to keeping W4, which doesn't help.\n\nJ1, J3 \u2014 Dragons \u2014 worth keeping for potential pung (Dragon Pung = 2 pts each, Two Dragon Pungs = 6 pts).\n\nF4 is your **Seat Wind** (North), so pung would give 2 pts.\n\nSo honors have potential.\n\nWhereas W4 is useless singleton.\n\nAlso, W4: unshown 2 \u2014 so not rare, but not critical.\n\n**Final choice: discard W4**\n\nIt\u2019s the only non-suit/non-honor tile.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding W4 removes the only Character suit tile not in a meld (though the meld is separate), simplifying hand toward Half Flush potential. Keeping various Dots and honors offers flexibility. Other discards would retain a mixed-suit liability.\n\nReason: W4 is a singleton from a different suit and hinders pursuit of Half Flush or Full Flush. Removing it improves hand cohesion and increases chances of forming higher-scoring patterns involving Dots and Honors.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
